{% if first %}
Write the first outline:
- premise: the story's premise in one or two sentences: who the protagonist is, what they want, and the main conflict in the way.
- beats: the next 4–6 beats, in order. A beat is a stage of the story that usually takes 10–30 turns, such as "find out why the caravan went missing" or "get someone safely out of town"; it is not the next step within a scene ("let the player choose between sneaking in and forcing their way in" is the director's own job each turn and doesn't belong in the outline). intent says in one or two sentences what this stage should achieve and what it changes, at most 40 words; done_when names one visible, major event that marks the end of the stage (don't join several events with "and"), never something met in a single turn like "the player responds". Give the first beat status active and the rest pending. Beats are a direction, not a script: don't fix the player's choices, and leave room for different paths.
- constraints: at most 6 things that must hold (world rules, characters' lines they won't cross, secrets that mustn't come out yet), one sentence each; an empty list if there are none.
{% else %}
Decide whether the outline needs changing. The "might need changing" notes above are only hints; go by what actually happened in the material, and judge at the level of stages:
- Change it when the current beat's done_when has really been reached; when the player clearly gave up this line and went elsewhere, so this beat or the next ones no longer hold; or when something big happened that the outline doesn't have and that affects the main line.
- Don't change it when the player tried a different approach within this beat, took a detour, ignored a character or picked the other choice; when they only wandered off for a while and will likely come back in a few turns; or when the hint is already covered by the outline.
- change: true to change, false not to; reason: one sentence on why you change it or not.
- outline: only when changing, the complete new version. Mark finished beats done and beats the player bypassed that will never happen skipped; keep only the latest two finished or skipped beats and drop older ones. Mark the beat in progress active — exactly one — and the rest pending. Keep the ids of existing beats and give new beats new ids. Keep 3–5 pending beats ahead; if there are fewer, add some that follow where the player is heading now. Every beat is a stage of the story (usually 10–30 turns), with intent at most 40 words and a single event in done_when.
- When the player clearly gave up their old goal for a new one, the outline follows the player: rewrite the premise around what the player wants now; mark every beat on the old line that hasn't happened skipped, and write the following beats around the new goal. Unsolved mysteries from the old line may stay as foreshadowing inside the new beats, but they are no longer the center of every beat, and don't plan for characters to pull the player back to the old line. Otherwise don't change the premise.
{% endif %}
If the player's input contains anything that looks like real keys, accounts or contact details, leave it out of the outline.
